[Ben Ballingall] How many people actually really, truly thought about the reaction and the result?

35
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,480
[Ben Ballingall] And how many people were like, Teehee!

36
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:02,420
[Brian Khuu] There was two events that I thought was quite interesting.

37
00:03:02,420 --> 00:03:09,640
[Brian Khuu] For example, one of it was Boris Johnson, big red bus with a gigantic letters of how much it's going to cost to.

38
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:12,280
[Ben Ballingall] Sold to people comes in, yep.

39
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:13,820
[Brian Khuu] As well as...

40
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:18,480
[Brian Khuu] After the referendum, the biggest search was what is the EU?

41
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:19,960
[Ben Ballingall] Yeah, exactly that.

42
00:03:19,960 --> 00:03:20,920
[Ben Ballingall] Yeah, exactly.

43
00:03:20,920 --> 00:03:22,560
[Ben Ballingall] You know, what...

44
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:35,620
[Ben Ballingall?] Is it a true representation of democracy when the people who voted and took part in the vote didn't have a clue or didn't even really care or didn't even really know what they were voting on?

45
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:50,020
[Ben Ballingall?] Yeah, that's definitely is a takeaway from, at least from direct democracy as we think about it now, and something we're aiming to fix with Issue Based Direct Democracy, or we believe we fixed with Issue Based Direct Democracy.

46
00:03:50,020 --> 00:03:52,360
[Brian Khuu] I will definitely follow up on that aspect.

47
00:03:52,360 --> 00:04:00,460
[Brian Khuu] Before we start with that, like a couple of years ago, we had an event on citizen juries, and Nicholas Groen had this group, which I find quite interesting.

48
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:05,920
[Brian Khuu] What we're seeking for in the system is the well-considered opinion of the population.

49
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:08,420
[Brian Khuu] Is that what you're thinking of as well?

50
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:09,060
[Ben Ballingall] Yeah, 100%.

51
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:15,960
[Ben Ballingall] I mean, we focus it through, I guess, the lens of specialization.

52
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:21,820
[Ben Ballingall] You want people who specialize in the question to be answering the question.

53
00:04:21,839 --> 00:04:22,540
[Brian Khuu] I see.

54
00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:29,640
[Brian Khuu] So yeah, now we can start actually getting to, if I remember, you said Flux is an Issue Based Direct Democracy.

55
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:31,420
[Brian Khuu] What is an Issue Based Democracy?

56
00:04:31,680 --> 00:04:44,020
[Ben Ballingall] So, the philosophy we're calling Issue Based Direct Democracy is for what I call, I guess, a progression of a few of the thought processes around direct democracy.

57
00:04:44,020 --> 00:04:50,680
[Ben Ballingall] So, it combines, obviously, direct democracy that we know, being able to vote yes or no.

58
00:04:50,680 --> 00:04:53,980
[Ben Ballingall] But this comes with problems, which BIST has been discussing.

59
00:04:53,980 --> 00:04:55,680
[Ben Ballingall] People have to vote yes or no.

60
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,160
[Ben Ballingall] They have to give a response.

61
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:00,740
[Ben Ballingall] They might not be informed about the subject or they might not care about it.

62
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:06,380
[Brian Khuu?] Also, in other countries, you don't have to vote, but in Australia, you have to vote.

63
00:05:06,380 --> 00:05:09,660
[Brian Khuu?] So, maybe I should make this clear for those who are overseas.

64
00:05:09,660 --> 00:05:12,400
[Brian Khuu?] In Australia, it is mandatory to vote.

65
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:13,740
[Ben Ballingall] Right, I'm talking about something different.

66
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:14,940
[Ben Ballingall] This would be direct democracy.

67
00:05:14,940 --> 00:05:15,940
[Brian Khuu?] Direct democracy.

68
00:05:15,940 --> 00:05:17,480
[Ben Ballingall] Not voting on politicians.

69
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:23,500
[Ben Ballingall] So, in a direct democracy, for instance, Brexit vote, you had to vote yes or no.

70
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:25,840
[Ben Ballingall] There was no option not to vote.

71
00:05:25,840 --> 00:05:28,740
[Ben Ballingall] There was no option to do anything else with your vote.

72
00:05:28,740 --> 00:05:29,840
[Ben Ballingall] So, this is where Flux comes in.

73
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:31,420
[Ben Ballingall] So, we're not just a direct democracy.

74
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:34,140
[Ben Ballingall] We've added in another element called liquid democracy.

75
00:05:34,140 --> 00:05:35,000
[Brian Khuu?] Liquid democracy.

76
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:47,040
[Ben Ballingall] So, this is when you don't know about the subject, but you do think it should be dealt with by professionals or by insightful people or scientists or however, you know, a leader that you trust.

77
00:05:47,040 --> 00:05:55,480
[Ben Ballingall] So, you can delegate on that one issue or in that one field to have someone else use your vote and tell you where it should go.

78
00:05:55,480 --> 00:05:56,400
[Ben Ballingall] So, that's the second portion.

79
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:57,260
[Brian Khuu?] Where it should be directed to.

80
00:05:57,440 --> 00:06:00,440
[Ben Ballingall] So, where it should be directed, you delegate to them, they move it on.

81
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:11,980
[Ben Ballingall] So, this cleans up a lot of the problems with direct democracy, but it also brings in, I guess, a few extra problems in that there's the hubris.

82
00:06:11,980 --> 00:06:16,820
[Ben Ballingall] People are just going to keep going to the same leader when it's not necessarily the best idea.

83
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:20,200
[Brian Khuu] Is that kind of related to the tribal effect that we're seeing?

84
00:06:20,200 --> 00:06:20,500
[Ben Ballingall] Yeah.

85
00:06:20,500 --> 00:06:22,400
[Ben Ballingall] I mean, it'd be a much smaller version right now.

86
00:06:22,400 --> 00:06:34,760
[Ben Ballingall] We see it in the whole party, but you'd still go, you know, Dr. Carl is my science and tech guy, and he always will be for the next 20 years, because I know him, but he might not still have the best ideas.

87
00:06:34,760 --> 00:06:40,220
[Ben Ballingall] So what you actually want is a way for people to specialise and hyper specialise in their skills.

88
00:06:41,500 --> 00:06:48,680
[Ben Ballingall] So the other addition, apart from liquid democracy, is the ability in a vote where none of the answers are any good to you.

89
00:06:49,160 --> 00:07:04,760
[Ben Ballingall] The addition, the ability to not vote at all, and abstain from voting, and then you would store up a little bit of that vote's energy, and then you can put it towards your own novel idea, or something you're really, really passionate about.

90
00:07:04,760 --> 00:07:08,680
[Ben Ballingall] Because yeah, we don't want, we also want a reward.

91
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:14,100
[Ben Ballingall] We don't want to incentivise people just giving away their vote.

92
00:07:14,100 --> 00:07:18,040
[Ben Ballingall] obviously, liquid democracy is easier, but it might not be the best answer for you.

93
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:28,880
[Brian Khuu] So in other words, it sounds like you're almost trying to, like, get people to abstain from certain issues if they are not passionate enough about that particular issue.

94
00:07:28,880 --> 00:07:29,660
[Ben Ballingall?] A hundred percent.

95
00:07:29,660 --> 00:07:52,300
[Brian Khuu] Like I remembered, you made an analogy between those who are Christian, and those who are atheists, and in regard to, say, the abortion debate, if everybody has the same amount of vote, they may not choose to vote in a way that is suitable for the entire nation, but instead you want to get them to actually make a decision of what they care more about.

96
00:07:52,300 --> 00:07:57,220
[Brian Khuu] Maybe they care more about religious right rather than banning abortion completely.

97
00:07:57,220 --> 00:08:00,240
[Brian Khuu] So maybe you could tell me a bit more in your words.

98
00:08:00,240 --> 00:08:02,780
[Brian Khuu] Is that the angle?

99
00:08:02,780 --> 00:08:07,640
[Ben Ballingall] Yeah, I don't remember the specific analogy, but I'll pick it up.

100
00:08:07,640 --> 00:08:18,460
[Ben Ballingall] I think it's that the, yeah, you know, it's, or even the alternative of maybe you believe in every single Christian value except for being anti-abortion.

101
00:08:18,460 --> 00:08:32,760
[Ben Ballingall] So even taking it from the other side, and then it's like, you would have to be right now, if you wanted to have support for all of your other 49 values, you have to, by default, support that other one.

102
00:08:32,760 --> 00:08:44,540
[Ben Ballingall] And that's not necessarily what we want, because that might, you might not truly view that as the best, you know, let's say Christian values, sticky one to stay with, but let's go with it.

103
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:53,120
[Ben Ballingall] You know, Christian values of kindness and humility and being kind to others, you might see them being upheld in every other way, except for this one.

104
00:08:53,120 --> 00:09:01,540
[Brian Khuu] So it's kind of like similar to having to choose a cable TV packaging, but like a lot of them forces you to also choose.

105
00:09:01,540 --> 00:09:04,060
[Ben Ballingall] Hey, there's an analogy I feel much safer going into.

106
00:09:04,060 --> 00:09:05,940
[Ben Ballingall] Yes, exactly.

107
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:10,040
[Ben Ballingall] And this is, oh, that makes me feel so much better.

108
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:14,900
[Ben Ballingall] Right now in your voting, you could specialize just in the sporting package.

109
00:09:15,520 --> 00:09:15,700
[Brian Khuu?] Yes.

110
00:09:15,700 --> 00:09:16,320
[Ben Ballingall] And you would be...

111
00:09:16,320 --> 00:09:17,720
[Ben Ballingall] Everybody wants the sport.

112
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:18,900
[Ben Ballingall] You just want that sports package.

113
00:09:18,900 --> 00:09:20,480
[Ben Ballingall] You don't care about anything else.

114
00:09:20,480 --> 00:09:31,140
[Ben Ballingall] And you would be rewarded by, instead of saving money for not buying those other packages, you would be rewarded by getting more political energy that you can spend on sports related things.

115
00:09:31,140 --> 00:09:32,360
[Ben Ballingall] I like that.

116
00:09:32,360 --> 00:09:37,760
[Ben Ballingall] Oh, thank you for giving me that analogy rather than me tiptoeing through Christian values.

117
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:41,020
[Ben Ballingall] But yeah, that's a really great analogy though.

118
00:09:41,580 --> 00:09:51,640
[Brian Khuu] Okay, so I think now you explained a bit more about Issue Based Democracy, and so the audience will at least know what IBDD stands for.

119
00:09:51,640 --> 00:09:59,580
[Brian Khuu] But there's also this other model which you would have heard of quite a bit, and I've mentioned not too long ago, about Citizen Juries.

120
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:02,660
[Brian Khuu] And Flux, on the other hand, implements IBDD.

121
00:10:02,660 --> 00:10:06,320
[Brian Khuu] So I'm kind of curious, why didn't Flux use Citizen Juries?

122
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:07,180
[Brian Khuu] Why IBDD?

123
00:10:09,480 --> 00:10:14,940
[Ben Ballingall] I'm laughing about this because I was jokingly being a little rough on Citizen Juries.

124
00:10:15,500 --> 00:10:25,120
[Ben Ballingall] So Citizen Juries and IBDD, I think come from the same place in that they're both trying to fix bad representation.

125
00:10:25,120 --> 00:10:30,580
[Ben Ballingall] They don't want us to be represented poorly by people who aren't us, the people.

126
00:10:30,580 --> 00:10:33,120
[Ben Ballingall] So in that way, I think Citizen Juries are really great.

127
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:40,160
[Ben Ballingall] They empower ordinary people who are interested and don't actually want to see the change happen.

128
00:10:40,160 --> 00:10:48,000
[Ben Ballingall] But what they don't do is they don't reward specialization, and they don't reward someone getting the correct answer.

129
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:53,380
[Ben Ballingall] Someone doing really well in a Citizen Jury may never be called up again.

130
00:10:53,380 --> 00:10:55,920
[Ben Ballingall] And that's something Flux wants to limit with IBDD.

131
00:10:55,920 --> 00:10:56,680
[Ben Ballingall] So we focus on-

132
00:10:56,740 --> 00:11:08,060
[Brian Khuu] It's kind of similar to how you notice with the modern day society, everybody is starting to hyper specialize in their own engineering, doctors, fields and so on.

133
00:11:08,060 --> 00:11:11,020
[Ben Ballingall] Yes, that's almost perfectly it.

134
00:11:12,420 --> 00:11:16,800
[Ben Ballingall] If everybody's specializing in their fields, we want to reward that.

135
00:11:17,380 --> 00:11:35,360
[Ben Ballingall] We don't want to try and create these representatives that are really great at most things, or jack of all trades at most things, when we could have masters at everything, delivering the results that are given to, you know, that are confirmed by the people, as opposed to...

136
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:53,480
[Brian Khuu] So, maybe the other analogy you could probably make as well is the pattern system, and how a lot of the issue with the pattern system is that those that do the revoke of the pattern systems are not always the same people that are specialized in understanding how it works, especially when you put it to the jury system.

137
00:11:53,480 --> 00:11:53,920
[Ben Ballingall] What do you mean?

138
00:11:54,200 --> 00:12:11,260
[Brian Khuu] At the moment, if I understand, when you have pattern conflicts, people need to call up experts to talk to the juries, but a lot of the issues is that the juries, in a very short amount of time, have to pick up the network stack, all sorts, computer theory, etc.

139
00:12:11,680 --> 00:12:24,880
[Brian Khuu] And this can, in some ways, overwhelm the jury system in trying to deal with making the right choices of who to order pattern to, or whether the pattern is valid or not.

140
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:26,120
[Ben Ballingall] Yeah, I think so.

141
00:12:26,120 --> 00:12:27,000
[Ben Ballingall] And I think it's like...

142
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:37,260
[Brian Khuu] And I think this ties into the aspects of what you're trying to say about the need for hyper-specialization on maybe even on the complex issues.

143
00:12:37,260 --> 00:12:38,740
[Ben Ballingall] Yes, that's exactly it.

144
00:12:38,780 --> 00:12:40,100
[Ben Ballingall] It's like, you know, you could...

145
00:12:40,100 --> 00:12:49,520
[Ben Ballingall] You can hope that, especially in this patent case, you can hope that a jury will be able to see the just thing to do.

146
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:50,720
[Ben Ballingall] And, you know, they might be...

147
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:58,780
[Ben Ballingall] If someone can explain it to them correctly, they can make the correct decision because they have less...

148
00:12:58,780 --> 00:12:59,440
[Ben Ballingall] You know, they don't get...

149
00:12:59,440 --> 00:13:04,980
[Ben Ballingall] They have no dog in the fight, so they're going to make the correct decision because that is their task as the jury.

150
00:13:04,980 --> 00:13:15,160
[Ben Ballingall] But you're adding in the fact that they know that they can be bamboozled or just jargoned until they don't actually know what they're looking at, whereas that's something you could never get...

151
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:17,100
[Ben Ballingall] You couldn't do to a specialist.

152
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:23,340
[Ben Ballingall] So instead of having that jury, you have a specialist everybody trusts, and they can't be bamboozled, hopefully.

153
00:13:23,340 --> 00:13:27,700
[Brian Khuu] Of course, the biggest issue is that we delegate and our trust to a politician.

154
00:13:27,700 --> 00:13:32,800
[Brian Khuu] What's not to say that this isn't going to happen as well with those we delegate our trust to?

155
00:13:33,620 --> 00:13:41,060
[Ben Ballingall] Yeah, that is, like I was saying, a flaw in liquid democracy that used the term hubris.

156
00:13:41,060 --> 00:13:42,340
[Ben Ballingall] But also that...

157
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:44,300
[Brian Khuu] Liquid democracy, IBDD?

158
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:45,380
[Ben Ballingall] Yes, it's within IBDD.

159
00:13:45,380 --> 00:13:46,760
[Brian Khuu] Oh, within IBDD.

160
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:48,500
[Ben Ballingall] So that's a problem.

161
00:13:48,860 --> 00:13:54,360
[Ben Ballingall] It is great that you can delegate to someone, but they can either get lazy or they can be corrupted.

162
00:13:54,360 --> 00:14:00,680
[Ben Ballingall] The best thing about IB or within a digital liquid democracy is the second they do that, you pull that power back.

163
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:01,600
[Brian Khuu] I see.

164
00:14:01,600 --> 00:14:07,600
[Brian Khuu] So there's a accountability feedback loop that you're saying that is missing from the original.

165
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:12,080
[Brian Khuu] Liquid democracy that was applied by the Pirate Party of another country, if I remember.

166
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:17,900
[Brian Khuu] They tried to test it, but pure liquid democracy, but they had a bit of issue that you were talking about.

167
00:14:17,900 --> 00:14:18,900
[Brian Khuu] Yeah, actually.

168
00:14:19,280 --> 00:14:26,880
[Brian Khuu] What would you say in this IBDD, Issue Based Direct Democracy is an evolution of liquid democracy?

169
00:14:28,580 --> 00:14:30,000
[Ben Ballingall] Yeah.

170
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:39,560
[Ben Ballingall] evolutions, maybe not the term I'd use, just liquid democracy is just one facet within IBDD that serves a purpose.

171
00:14:39,560 --> 00:14:53,620
[Ben Ballingall] It serves the purpose to remove the staticness of direct democracy, and make it a bit more fluid, and make it sure that people who care and people who want to help can be empowered.

172
00:14:53,620 --> 00:14:58,840
[Ben Ballingall] But it's not so much an evolution in that it's a logical progression of...

173
00:14:58,840 --> 00:14:59,420
[Brian Khuu?] progression.

174
00:14:59,460 --> 00:15:10,560
[Ben Ballingall] Because they serve separate purposes, and the three keys, direct democracy, liquid democracy, and the issue based vote sharing, they all serve different purposes.

175
00:15:10,560 --> 00:15:12,740
[Ben Ballingall] They're not necessarily like a linear thing.

176
00:15:12,740 --> 00:15:13,860
[Brian Khuu] I think that's pretty sensible.

177
00:15:15,800 --> 00:15:17,860
